<p class="hint center">For planning only. Results depend on what you enter and your policy terms, and aren’t a quote, appraisal or legal advice. Nothing is stored.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>The pitch multiplier</h2>
<p>A pitched roof has more surface than the footprint under it. The multiplier is √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²). A 9/12 roof — common on older capes — carries about 25% more surface than its footprint; a 12/12 roof about 41% more.</p>
<h2>Comparing against the insurer’s estimate</h2>
<ul><li>Find total squares and the waste percentage in the roof section.</li>
<li>Differences above 5–10% are worth a closer look at the measurement report.</li>
<li>Look for steep and high-roof charges, plus starter, ridge, drip edge and ice &amp; water shield lines.</li></ul>
<h2>Where a footprint falls short</h2>
<p>Roofs with dormers, ells, attached barns or several pitches need plane-by-plane measuring. Use this as a sanity check alongside an aerial report or a tape measure.</p>

<h2>About this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is a square?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>One hundred square feet of roof surface. Many New Hampshire single-family roofs fall somewhere between 18 and 35 squares.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why does waste change by roof style?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Hips, valleys and dormers mean more cutting, so more material becomes scrap.</p></div></details></div>
